        "id": 109,
        "title": "What is Exhaustive Checking?",
        "category": "Advanced TypeScript",
        "language": "typescript",
        "explanation": "Exhaustive Checking is a pattern used alongside Discriminated Unions to mathematically guarantee that you have handled every possible case in a `switch` statement. \nYou do this by assigning the default case to the `never` type. If a developer adds a new type to the union later but forgets to add a `case` for it, the compiler will throw an error because the unhandled type will fall through to the `never` assignment.",
        "example": "Ensuring a switch statement handles all Redux actions or all Payment methods.",
        "code": "type TrafficLight = \"red\" | \"yellow\" | \"green\";\n\nfunction handleLight(light: TrafficLight) {\n    switch (light) {\n        case \"red\": return \"STOP\";\n        case \"yellow\": return \"SLOW\";\n        case \"green\": return \"GO\";\n        default:\n            // EXHAUSTIVE CHECK\n            // If someone adds \"blue\" to TrafficLight, TS will throw an error here!\n            const _exhaustiveCheck: never = light;\n            return _exhaustiveCheck;\n    }\n}",
