        "category": "Classes & OOP",
        "language": "typescript",
        "explanation": "Method Overloading allows multiple methods with the same name to be declared, but with different parameters. \nUnlike Java or C# where you write multiple physical function bodies, TypeScript only allows ONE physical implementation. You must write multiple **Type Signatures** (the overloads) stacked on top of a single, highly flexible **Implementation Signature**.",
        "example": "A function that can fetch a user by ID (number) OR by Username (string).",
        "code": "// 1. Overload Signature 1\nfunction getUser(id: number): object;\n// 2. Overload Signature 2\nfunction getUser(username: string): object;\n\n// 3. The Implementation (Must encompass all overloads)\nfunction getUser(identifier: number | string): object {\n    if (typeof identifier === \"number\") {\n        return { id: identifier, method: \"ByID\" };\n    } else {\n        return { name: identifier, method: \"ByUsername\" };\n    }\n}\n\ngetUser(1); // Matches Signature 1\ngetUser(\"alice\"); // Matches Signature 2\n// getUser(true); // ERROR: No overload matches this call",
        "tip": "The implementation signature is hidden from the consumer. They only see the specific Overload signatures in their IDE autocomplete.",
